analysisWe need to read the code and produce an assert statement corresponding to the comment starting with "@@@". The comment is within toArray method:

```java
public Object[] toArray() {
    Object[] result = new Object[size];
    int i = 0;
    for (Node<E> x = first; x != null; x = x.next)
        result[i++] = x.item;
    return result;
    // @@@ The length of the resulting array is equal to the size of the list.
}
```

We need to assert that the length of the resulting array equals the size of the list.

We need to refer to the returned array length and the size of the list. The method returns result. In the assert, we can use \result to refer to the returned value. The size of the list after the operation is unchanged (since toArray is not modifying). Use this.size or this.size()? The class field is 'size', but there is a public method size(). Use this.size()? It's a method that returns number of elements. So we can use this.size().

Thus assert: assert \result.length == this.size(); but need Java syntax: result is an Object[]; length property is .length.

In JML style, we can write: assert \result.length == this.size();.

Make sure to use correct syntax for array length: \result.length.

Thus final answer:

<code>assert \result.length == this.size();</code>

Check if need parentheses: not needed.

Thus final.assistantfinal<code>assert \result.length == this.size();</code>
